--- A function whose parameter is reassigned from a call that can fail
--- (`words = maybe_reversed[words]`) and which answers a one-item list of it
--- (`return [words]`), called only from inside another function, does not build
--- compiled: its answer is typed a list of values that can fail
--- (`crow build --ir`: `pure pieces(words: List(Str)) -> Result(List(Result(List(Str))))`),
--- while `[words]` holds the payload, so the generated `vec![words]` is a
--- Vec<Vec<String>> where a Vec<CrowResult<Vec<String>>> is expected.
---
--- Interpreted (crow run -i):  1
--- Compiled (crow run):
---     error[E0308]: mismatched types
---       --> src/main.rs:39:47
---     error: the compiled program does not build — the Rust compiler refused what Crow
---     accepted, a bug in Crow, not in this program (`crow run -i` runs it meanwhile)
--- Expected: 1 in both modes (the parameter is never a value that can fail, and a
--- failure of maybe_reversed is handed on by the assignment).
---
--- Builds and runs when: `pieces` is also (or only) called at the top level, the
--- result goes to a new local (`turned = maybe_reversed[words]`, `return [turned]`),
--- or the reassigning call cannot fail (`words = reverse[words]`). With a second
--- return of `[]` it fails the same; with another return that hands a list of lists
--- from a function (`return walk[…]`), the generated code converts that answer by
--- making an EMPTY Vec::<CrowResult<…>>::new() in its place — a wrong answer once
--- the other return no longer stops the build.
--- Found writing rosetta/weiler_atherton_polygon_clipping.co: a test driver calling
--- the clipper (whose polygons are reoriented first, `subject = turned[subject]`) from
--- a function did not build; the solution calls it at the top level.

fn maybe_reversed[words]
    if len[words] gt 9
        return error['too many']
    return reverse[words]

fn pieces[words]
    words = maybe_reversed[words]
    return [words]

fn show[words]
    result = pieces[words]
    log[len[result]]

show[['a', 'b']]
